{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And YHWH spoke to Moses, saying</b>. The commentators said (Radak, Sefer haShorashim, root אמר) that the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> throughout the Torah means 'to say to Israel' — the sense being that HaShem says to Moses: Say these words of mine to Israel. But here it means 'to say to Pharaoh.' However, the verse that says, 'Yesterday you said to me, saying' (Genesis 31:29), does not fit this interpretation, and likewise in many places where it is doubled: 'And YHWH spoke to Moses, saying: Speak to the children of Israel and say to them' (Leviticus 18:1–2), and likewise 'And YHWH said to Moses, saying: Speak to the children of Israel and say to them' (Numbers 15:37–38), 'And they said to all the congregation of the children of Israel, saying' (Numbers 14:7), 'And the children of Israel said to Moses, saying: Behold, we perish' (Numbers 17:27), 'And I said to you at that time, saying' (Deuteronomy 1:9), and many similar cases. And likewise in this section (Exodus 7:8–9), 'And YHWH said to Moses and to Aaron, saying: When Pharaoh speaks to you, saying' — this is not 'saying to others' in the correct sense. What seems correct to me is that the word לֵאמֹר indicates the clarity of the matter in every instance: 'And YHWH spoke to Moses' — in complete speech, not doubtful speech, and not by hint of a matter. Therefore this expression persists throughout the Torah, for the prophecy of Moses is 'mouth to mouth I speak with him, and not in riddles' (Numbers 12:8). And Laban said thus to Jacob: 'Yesterday the deity said to me, saying' (Genesis 31:29) — in clear speech, 'I will not harm you,' for had it not been so, I would have done evil to you. And likewise, 'And the children of Israel said to Moses (and Aaron), saying' (Numbers 17:27) — they were saying to them thus explicitly and crying out to them with uncovered face, for the root meaning is the clarity of the matter, sometimes placed before and sometimes after, as in 'They speak, saying, to those who despise me' (Jeremiah 23:17)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Radak is cited by name; his work Sefer haShorashim (Book of Roots) is referenced by title and root entry. The verse citation abbreviations have been normalized to standard chapter:verse format."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:03:47.951465155Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}